  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe Thunderstorms are possible this evening\nas a strong cold front moves through.\n\nSkies remain mostly sunny across the majority of the forecast\narea, which has allowed temperatures to climb into the lower to\nmid 80s. Dewpoints have also risen into the 60s to near 70\nwithin southerly flow. This unseasonably warm and humid airmass\ncoupled with cooling temperatures aloft are allowing instability\nto build over the area. Thunderstorms are in the process of \nforming along a strong cold front which is just starting to move\ninto western MD and the WV Panhandle. The coverage of storms is\nexpected to gradually increase as the front moves into an \nenvironment further east characterized by slightly higher \ninstability, with the greatest coverage of storms expected to \nthe east of the Blue Ridge this evening where the highest \ndewpoints (upper 60s to near 70) are located.\n\nConfidence in the timing of the storms is very high since\nthey're forming directly along the front. The storms are located\nover Western MD and the WV Panhandle currently, should reach the\nBlue Ridge by around 5-6 PM, impact the I-95 corridor between 6\nand 8 PM, and then Southern MD shortly thereafter.\n\nIn terms of hazards, damaging winds appears to be the primary\nthreat, with ample flow in place just off the surface. With\naround 40-50 knots of effective bulk shear, some supercellular\nor bowing structures could be possible, which could locally\nenhance the threat for damaging winds. However, relatively weak\nlow-level lapse rates could be a limiting factor for damaging\nwinds. As thunderstorm coverage increases to the east of the\nBlue Ridge, we'll also be losing daytime heating, which will\nonly make the low-level lapse rates even poorer. While the\nenvironment doesn't appear to be the most favorable for\ntornadoes, a brief tornado or two can't be completely ruled \nout. The threat for severe thunderstorms will immediately come \nto an end once the front moves through, with winds picking up \nout of the northwest behind the front. A Severe Thunderstorm \nWatch remains in effect for the majority of the forecast are \nthrough 8 PM.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and/or freezing temperatures are possible\nthis weekend.\n\nStrong cold advection will ensue within northwesterly flow \nbehind the cold front tonight through the day tomorrow. Upslope\nshowers in the Alleghenies may even end as a few flakes of snow\nlate tonight, but no accumulation is expected. Temperatures are\nforecast to drop into the 40s by daybreak tomorrow (mid- upper \n30s mountains). A few showers could linger from Central Virginia\nto Southern Maryland tomorrow morning, but gradual clearing is \nexpected tomorrow afternoon as the upper trough axis progresses \nto our east and large scale subsidence starts to increase. \nTemperatures will be much cooler tomorrow, with highs in the 50s\n(40s mountains).\n\nHigh pressure will gradually build in from the west tomorrow\nnight as a nor'easter simultaneously starts to develop off the \nCarolina coastline. The main question tomorrow night is which \nlocations will decouple. The setup for radiational cooling will \nbe nearly ideal in locations that go calm, with clear skies \nexpected and dewpoints in the 20s. Temperatures will likely \nplummet into the 20s and lower 30s where winds go calm. \nConfidence is currently the highest for winds going calm to the \nwest of the Blue Ridge, with greater uncertainty with respect to\nthe winds further east as the nor'easter develops offshore. A \nFreeze Watch has been issued to the west of the Blue Ridge, \nwhere confidence is highest for sub-freezing temperatures. Some \nfrost or sub-freezing temperatures can't be ruled out further \neast, either. Forecast confidence decreases further moving into\nSunday. Forecast models show considerable spread with respect \nto the location of the aforementioned nor'easter during that \ntime period. Some solutions keep the system offshore, while \nothers have it retrograding further westward, which could lead \nto impacts locally.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Nor'easter to develop off the Carolina coast late\nin the weekend lingers much of next week.\n\nPart of the upper trough that swings through tonight into \nSaturday will cutoff along the front stalling to the south later\nthis weekend. This result in the development of a nor'easter \noff the Carolina coast. This system then meanders offshore/near \nthe coast, but guidance varies widely on its placement and \nstrength. Since it will be cut off from the main steering flow, \nit could persist much of next week. Northeast breezes and \noccasional rain chances are possible, with the highest chances \nfurther east.",
